.TH PINNED 1 "2026-08-27" "pinned" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pinned \- review-and-pin trust gate for git repositories and files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pinned
.I action
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B pinned \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pinned
keeps root-owned approval records behind a human gate.
An approval ceremony shows the human exactly what is being trusted
(the diff since the last approval, or a file's bytes), then records a
pin \(em a git commit hash for a repository, a content digest for a
file \(em under root custody in
.IR /var/db/pinned .
Verification verbs answer against those records with quiet, stable
exit codes, so consumers (build gates, launchers, lanes) can fail
closed without re-deciding trust.
.PP
Actions marked
.B (sudo)
run as root.
.BR review ,
.BR tombstone ,
.BR rekey ,
.B declare
and
.B setup
self-elevate when invoked unprivileged: they re-exec the installed,
root-owned binary under
.BR sudo (8),
whose sudoers entry is digest-pinned to the installed bytes (a swapped
binary makes sudo refuse).
There is no NOPASSWD \(em sudo authenticates every invocation.
The
.B signer
and
.B ignorable
add/remove ceremonies self-elevate the same way; their
.B list
forms, like every reading verb, run unprivileged.
.PP
.B The pre\-sudo handoff.
Every self-elevating verb discloses the exact command it is about to
hand to
.BR sudo (8),
indented on its own line, and closes with a gate naming what the
authentication buys:
.PP
.nf
    sudo \-\- /usr/local/bin/pinned review /home/u/src/foo

  next: review 1 repo as root; Enter runs the line above \(pc n stops
.fi
.PP
The displayed line is the argv, token for token \(em nothing is added,
elided, or reworded for the display \(em and it is printed
unconditionally, because on a host without a root-side echo it is the
only account of the command the human gets before authenticating.
Enter,
.BR y ,
and
.B yes
run it;
.B n
stops there, printing
.RB \(dq stopped; \(dq
and the state the verb leaves untouched, and exiting 2.
End of input stops the same way.
Anything else is not an answer at all: the gate asks again, without
echoing what was typed, so a typo cannot buy an authentication.
Continuing still decides nothing on its own \(em the ceremony's own
.RB \(dq [y/N] \(dq
comes later, as root, and nothing is recorded until it is answered.
.B \-\-yes
skips the gate (and rides through into the root-side argv, where it
decides nothing); off a tty, a verb whose gate cannot be answered
refuses with exit 2 rather than elevate unasked.
The gate applies to
.BR review ,
.BR tombstone ,
.BR rekey ,
.BR declare ,
and the
.B signer
and
.B ignorable
add/remove ceremonies;
.B setup
takes its own confirmation instead, before any authentication.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Repository approval
.TP
.B review \fIrepo\fP... [\fB\-\-tag\fP \fItag\fP] [\fB\-\-trust\fP] [\fB\-\-step\fP] [\fB\-\-backward\fP|\fB\-\-diverged\fP] [\fB\-\-yes\fP]
(sudo) Review the diff since the last pin (the full tree on a first
approval), approve, write the pin.
.I repo
must be the work-tree root: a pin names a commit of the whole
repository, so a subdirectory is refused with the root named
(the same rule for every verb that takes a repository).
Before any sudo prompt, an unprivileged preview shows each repo, the
commit about to be reviewed, and the commits since the last approval;
the authoritative review re-derives everything from the object store
as root, after sudo.
Each commit in that listing carries its own changed-file, insertion and
deletion counts, so a subject line cannot pass a large commit off as a
small one.
A merge shows "\-" for all three: git keeps no per-commit diff for it,
and the review below covers the merged content anyway.
.IP
That preview closes with the pre-sudo handoff: the exact command, then a
gate counting the repos that still need a ceremony \(em a repo already at
its pin was skipped above and is never promised here.
.IP
Several repos run as sequential ceremonies under one authentication:
each is displayed and confirmed on its own, a decline skips only that
repo, and a summary line closes the batch.
.IP
A repo record names its path exactly as a file record does (see
.BR FILES ).
When the ceremony finds the slot stale for the path as it is now
spelled \(em the record naming a former spelling, or the slot
directory still wearing one (a case\-only rename on a
case\-insensitive volume) \(em it repairs both at entry: the record is
re\-stated with the same rev and the directory renamed, announced on
screen, logged as
.BR respell ,
and not gated behind the y/N, because the rev is unchanged and the
filesystem already resolves both spellings to this slot; the gate
guards
.I which
rev is trusted, and this restores the slot\-name invariant.
An already\-pinned repo with a stale slot still gets its ceremony (the
preview says why), then reports the pin as the no\-op it is.
.B status
reports either half of the staleness before any ceremony.
.IP
Every diff opens behind its own gate line; answering
.B n
there declines without opening it \(em the pin is unchanged, the
outcome is logged as a skip, and a batch counts it as declined and
moves on.
Enter,
.BR y ,
and
.B yes
open the diff;
.B n
is the only other answer, and end of input takes the skip.
Anything else is named back and the gate asks again.
Approving a repo without its review is never offered.
The gate states the delta's size: the commit count, or, when the pin and
the candidate share no history in the checkout, the diff's own totals
.RI ( N " files +" A " \-" D ),
since a commit count there would be the candidate's whole history.
When the pager closes, a closing rule
.RB ( "\-\-\- end of diff: ... \-\-\-" )
stays on screen above the confirmation and gives the diff's totals, so
the scrollback records that a diff was shown and how large it was.
A file pin's closing rule gives its line counts.
.BR \-\-tag ,
.BR \-\-signed\-tag ,
and
.B \-\-trust
bind to a single repo \(em a batch takes plain review approvals only, and
so do the ancestry declarations below.
.IP
.B The ancestry lattice.
Before anything is displayed, the candidate commit is classified against
the recorded pin over the commit graph \(em never by parsing a version
string, because tag and branch names are repo content and version sort is
a convention:
.RS
.TP
.B equal
The candidate is the pin: a no-op, reported as already pinned.
.TP
.B forward
The pin is an ancestor of the candidate: the ordinary approval.
.TP
.B backward
The candidate is an ancestor of the pin: the commits between are being
un-approved.
.TP
.B diverged
Neither: the pinned line of history is being abandoned (a rewrite, or a
side branch).
.RE
.IP
Forward proceeds.
Backward and diverged refuse unless the human declares them with
.B \-\-backward
or
.BR \-\-diverged ,
and a declaration reality contradicts refuses too, naming the class that
actually holds \(em declared, never inferred.
A declaration on a forward candidate is refused as well: there is nothing
to override.
A first approval has no pin, so it classifies nothing and takes no
declaration.
.IP
A declared move is loud.
The ceremony opens with a full-caps alarm banner naming the class and both
revs, then shows what the move covers in the direction that makes it
readable: for backward, the commits being un-approved (the reversed
range); for diverged, the merge base, the commits leaving the pinned line
and the commits arriving on the new one.
The authoritative diff still runs, pin to candidate \(em an honest
rendering of what trust is moving to.
The classification is mirrored in the pre-sudo preview, so an undeclared
non-forward move costs no authentication.
.IP
The lattice governs ceremonies \(em the movement of a pin \(em and
nothing else:
.BR verify ,
.B status
and
.B rev
answer about a pin already recorded and are unaffected.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-tag " tag"
Review and pin the tag's commit, not HEAD.
.TP
.B \-\-trust
Skip a first approval's full-tree review, loudly.
Later approvals always show the diff.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip the pre-sudo gate, and nothing else: every repo is still displayed
and confirmed as root.
Required off a tty, where the gate cannot be answered.
.TP
.B \-\-step
A reading aid for a large delta: instead of one diff of the whole
delta, walk the commits since the pin oldest-first \(em one diff and
one confirmation per commit.
The walk ends at HEAD, or at the commit
.B \-\-tag
names; the declared release name is written only when the walk
actually approves that commit \(em stopping early leaves the slot
rev-only, and the summary says so.
The pin advances at every yes, so each checkpoint is a coherent,
deployable record: stopping midway rests the pin at the last commit
actually read, and resuming later is an ordinary
.B review
over whatever remains.
A decline stops the walk rather than skipping the commit \(em trust is
a tree state, so approving commit N would silently carry N\-1's
changes in with it.
An
.B n
at a step's diff gate stops the walk the same way, before the diff
opens.
.IP
The walk always closes with a summary: how many commits were approved,
where the pin came to rest, the per-commit counts for the approved
range, one aggregate
.RB ( total: )
line for the whole sitting, and how many commits remain.
That aggregate is the point of the summary \(em reading one commit at
a time covers the same lines as one whole-delta diff, but it fragments
the attention that would notice mayhem split across individually
innocent commits.
The exit status is 0 if the pin advanced at all (a later decline does
not undo an earlier yes) and 2 if nothing was approved.
.IP
The single whole-delta diff remains the default and the norm.
.B \-\-step
takes one repo and refuses every combination it cannot mean:
.BR \-\-file ,
.BR \-\-trust ,
.BR \-\-signed\-tag ,
.BR \-\-backward ,
.BR \-\-diverged ,
several repos, a batch review, and a first approval (which reviews
the full tree \(em there is nothing to step from).
A backward or diverged endpoint is refused by the lattice before the walk
begins: the commits a walk replays only exist going forward, and the
whole-delta diff of a declared move renders that situation honestly.
.TP
.B \-\-backward
Declare that the candidate is an ancestor of the current pin \(em that
the commits between are being un-approved.
Refused unless that is what the commit graph actually shows.
.TP
.B \-\-diverged
Declare that the candidate is not on the pinned line of history \(em that
the line is being abandoned for a rewrite or a side branch.
Refused unless that is what the commit graph actually shows.
.IP
Both declarations bind to one repo and combine with neither each other,
.BR \-\-step ,
.BR \-\-trust ,
nor
.BR \-\-file .
They do combine with
.B \-\-signed\-tag
and
.BR \-\-tag :
a signature says who vouched, never which way the pin is moving, so a
replayed signed release of an older version is exactly what the lattice
exists to catch \(em and a deliberate, declared downgrade to a signed
release is the legitimate manual override it exists to allow.
.RE
.TP
.B review \fIrepo\fP \fB\-\-signed\-tag\fP \fItag\fP [\fB\-\-signed\-tag\fP \fItag\fP ...] [\fB\-\-tag\fP \fItag\fP] [\fB\-\-yes\fP]
(sudo) Verify each signed tag against root-owned allowed signers (the
slot's own
.IR signers/allowed_signers ,
else the user tier's
.IR policy/allowed_signers ).
All tags must name the same commit; approve, pin.
.B \-\-tag
may add an unsigned name (a clean release name) that must name the
same commit.
.SS Declaring a release name
.TP
.B declare \fIrepo\fP (\fB\-\-release\fP \fIname\fP | \fB\-\-no\-release\fP) [\fB\-\-yes\fP]
(sudo) Name the release a pinned rev already is \(em or clear that
name.
The declaration is the slot's
.B release
record: the release name a deployer
syncs the input's
.B ref=
to, after cross-checking that the live tag still names the approved
rev.
A review that moves the pin already writes it
.RB ( \-\-tag ,
.BR \-\-signed\-tag ),
and a plain review clears it; this verb is how a slot that is already
pinned gains a declaration \(em or gives one up \(em without waiting
for the next release.
.IP
A declaration may only name a tag that already resolves to the slot's
pinned rev \(em a deployer's own cross-check, run at declaration time,
so declare can never write a record a deployer would refuse.
It cannot change what is built: the rev is the trust decision and
declare never touches it \(em
.B ref=
only tells nix where to fetch a rev it verifies anyway, so a wrong ref
is a fetch failure, never a different build.
It cannot invent provenance: "this rev is also known as \fIname\fP" is
checked, not taken.
And a name that moves later fails closed, because a deployer re-runs
the same check on every sync.
A tag missing from the checkout, a tag naming another commit, a name
outside the declared-name grammar, and an unpinned or tombstoned slot
all refuse before anything is written.
.IP
Signatures are deliberately out of scope: unsigned annotated and
lightweight tags work, because the tag carries no authority here
beyond "this name currently points at the already-approved commit",
which is checked directly.
Repos that sign keep the stronger path they already have:
.B review \-\-signed\-tag
writes the declaration as a side effect, and a deployer may offer it
automatically.
Nothing should promote a rev-only slot on its own \(em tag names are
repo content, and content must never choose the shape of a slot; once a
slot is declared, the tagged and signed routes maintain the name at
every release.
.IP
.B \-\-no\-release
clears the declaration and the slot goes rev-only: a deployer
stops managing the input's
.BR ref= ,
which keeps whatever it says.
Both flags state a desired end state, and success means the slot now
matches it: a name the slot already declares answers
.RB \(dq "already declared" \(dq
and a rev-only slot asked for
.B \-\-no\-release
answers
.RB \(dq "already rev-only" \(dq ,
exit 0 either way, so a provisioning script may re-run the verb.
The no-op still costs the sudo prompt: the pre-sudo side validates the
arguments and never reads root-owned slot state.
.IP
The pre-sudo gate reads
.RB \(dq "next: declare the release name as root" \(dq
(for
.BR \-\-no\-release :
.RB \(dq "clear the declaration" \(dq);
.B \-\-yes
skips it and leaves the root-side confirmation untouched.
.B status
reports the declaration in all three of its states \(em declared and
live, rev-only, or stale (the live tag no longer names the pin), the
last being the early warning for a deploy that would refuse.
.SS File approval
.TP
.B review \-\-file \fIpath\fP [\fB\-\-baseline\fP \fIcopy\fP] [\fB\-\-ignore\-json\-key\fP \fIkey\fP ...] [\fB\-\-file\fP \fIpath\fP ...] [\fB\-\-baseline\-store\fP \fIdir\fP] [\fB\-\-algo\fP \fIname\fP] [\fB\-\-store\fP] [\fB\-\-yes\fP]
(sudo) File-pin ceremony: each file is frozen, displayed root-side,
confirmed, and its hash recorded.
Multiple
.B \-\-file
arguments share one authentication.
The pre-sudo handoff counts the files named on the command line
.RB ( "next: review 2 files as root" );
.B \-\-yes
skips that gate and no other, so each file is still displayed and
confirmed as root.
Each file's display opens behind its own gate line; answering
.B n
there declines without opening it \(em record unchanged, counted as
declined.
.IP
The ceremony re-states
.B \-\-ignore\-json\-key
and
.B \-\-store
every time: an approval without them clears the declaration and drops
the stored copy, loudly.
.IP
This ceremony is also the repair for a record left behind by a
respelled path.  On a case\-insensitive volume a case\-only rename
(staticdrop \(-> StaticDrop) leaves both spellings resolving to one
slot, whose record still names the old spelling \(em
.B verify
refuses it with exit 17, and
.B rekey
cannot express the move because old and new are one directory.
Reviewing the file re\-states the record for the path as it is now
spelled; identical bytes do not short\-circuit to \(lqalready
approved\(rq while the recorded path disagrees.  When the slot
directory itself still wears the old spelling, approving also renames
it to match (announced before the confirm, logged as
.BR respell ),
so listings decode the path the record names.  Nothing here folds
case: on a case\-sensitive filesystem two spellings are two files, two
slots, two records, exactly as before.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline " copy"
(after its
.BR \-\-file )
Diff against a copy that must re-hash to the prior record.
When both sides parse as JSON and
.B jq
is available, one summary line above the diff names the changed keys with
their added/removed counts (e.g.
.IR "keys: permissions.ask(+2)" ).
It is computed from whole-document comparison \(em a summary, never a
per-hunk claim \(em and is silently absent for non-JSON content, malformed
JSON, or a missing
.BR jq ;
every changed line is still shown either way, and the recorded digest is
taken from the frozen bytes.
The diff's other side is chosen in
.BR verify 's
order: the slot's own stored copy when it has one, then this
.BR \-\-baseline ,
then the
.B \-\-baseline\-store
entry; each is used only if it re-hashes to the prior record, and with
no usable copy the whole file is shown.
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline\-store " dir"
(once per ceremony)
A directory of witnesses kept by the caller, laid out as
.IR dir /\fIslot\-name\fP/\fIdigest\fP
(the slot name as
.B pinned slot
prints it, the digest as the record holds it).
A
.B \-\-file
without its own
.B \-\-baseline
diffs against the entry for its slot and its prior digest, when that
entry re-hashes to the record.
Both name parts are pinned's own facts, so the command line names one
directory instead of one machine-derived path per file.
.TP
.BI \-\-ignore\-json\-key " key"
(after its
.BR \-\-file ,
repeatable)
Declare a dotted JSON key whose later drift
.B verify
tolerates, answering exit code 5.
The key must be granted for that path by the ignorable policy or the
ceremony refuses (see
.BR ignorable ).
.TP
.BI \-\-algo " name"
Hash algorithm; see
.B show
for the recognized names.
.TP
.B \-\-store
Keep a copy of the approved bytes in the slot \(em the "approved"
witness: what the ignored-key comparison reads, and what
.B pinned cat
serves.
.RE
.TP
.B tombstone \fIpath\fP [\-\-yes]
(sudo) Retire a pinned file that is gone.
If it reappears,
.B verify
refuses until it is re-approved.
The pre-sudo gate reads
.RB \(dq "next: tombstone the record as root" \(dq;
.B \-\-yes
skips it and leaves the root-side confirmation untouched.
.TP
.B rekey \fIold\fP \fInew\fP [\-\-yes]
(sudo) Re-key an existing record to the path its content has moved to.
The record travels verbatim \(em same commit hash or digest, same
declarations \(em so this is a rename, never a re-approval, and it
cannot widen what is trusted.
It follows a move that has already happened: \fIold\fP must be gone, or
the ceremony refuses.
.IP
Before anything is displayed, the machine checks that the content now at
\fInew\fP is what the record already names.
A repository record requires a usable work tree at \fInew\fP, named at
its root, whose object store holds the pinned commit \(em a work tree
that does not is a different repository wearing the new name.
A file record requires a regular file at \fInew\fP that re-hashes to the
recorded digest; a file differing only in keys the slot already declares
ignored (and the policy still grants) is accepted too, with the drifted
keys named, and the record is still carried over unchanged \(em the
tolerated drift is never re-recorded.
Anything else refuses.
.IP
The annotations travel with it: the declared
.BR tag ,
the
.B ignored.<format>
declaration, the stored
.B approved
witness and any per-slot
.BR signers/allowed_signers .
The new slot must be free \(em a live record, a tombstone, or per-slot
signer data already there all refuse, because records do not merge.
.IP
The new record is written first, then \fIold\fP is tombstoned rather
than deleted: content reappearing at a formerly trusted path must fail
closed (see
.BR tombstone ).
An interrupted
.B rekey
therefore leaves the old record live beside the new one, both naming the
same bytes, and re-running finishes the job.
.IP
A rename the filesystem does not consider a move is not a
.B rekey
matter: after a case\-only rename on a case\-insensitive volume both
spellings resolve to one path and one slot, so there is no \(lqold\(rq
to leave and no free slot to move into \(em the ceremony refuses, and
the repair is a review (see
.B review \-\-file
and exit 17 under EXIT STATUS).
.IP
The pre-sudo gate reads
.RB \(dq "next: re-key the record as root" \(dq;
.B \-\-yes
skips it and leaves the root-side confirmation untouched.
.SS Verification and serving
.TP
.B verify \fIpath\fP
File-pin verdict; a quiet exit-code contract for gates.
See EXIT STATUS.
.TP
.B verify \-\-emit \fIpath\fP
Verify, then print the verified bytes on stdout (nothing on failure)
\(em the parse-safely form.
Byte-exact only: never answers 5.
.TP
.B verify \-\-frozen \fIcopy\fP \fIpath\fP
Verify caller-frozen bytes against
.IR path 's
record.
.TP
.B verify [\-\-baseline \fIcopy\fP] \fIpath\fP
Bring your own witness for the ignored-key comparison \(em a copy that
must re-hash to the record before it is used; for a slot that keeps
none of its own (see
.BR "review \-\-store" ).
.TP
.B cat \fIpath\fP
The approved bytes on stdout, from root custody \(em no sudo, writes
nothing.
Re-hashes the slot's stored witness against the record before emitting
a byte, and prints nothing at all on any failure (see EXIT STATUS).
Serves the approved bytes even when the live file has drifted \(em
drift is
.BR verify 's
question, not this verb's.
.TP
.B rev \fIrepo\fP [\-\-release]
The pinned rev on stdout, from the repo slot's record \(em no sudo,
writes nothing; the repo counterpart of
.BR cat ,
and the one surface a consumer that syncs a flake input reads the pin
through.
Validates the record (declared VCS, digest shape, ownership and mode)
before printing, and prints nothing at all on any failure (see EXIT
STATUS).
With
.B \-\-release
prints the declared release name instead, under the declared\-name
grammar; whether the live tag still names the pin is the deployer's
cross\-check at deploy time, not a fact the record holds.
Answers about the record only: the checkout gates nothing here, and a
record whose checkout is gone still answers.
.SS Signing
.TP
.B sign \fIrepo\fP \fItag\fP
Create signed tag
.I tag
at the pinned hash \(em no sudo (needs your SSH agent, e.g. 1Password).
.TP
.B signer add [\-\-repo \fIpath\fP] (\-\-file \fIpubkey\fP | \-\-key \fIline\fP) [\-\-principal \fIid\fP] [\-\-yes]
(sudo) Allowed-signers ceremony: fingerprint, confirm, root-owned
write.
A bare public key needs
.B \-\-principal
(the label signature verification matches);
.B \-\-repo
targets the per-repo override.
The pre-sudo gate reads
.RB \(dq "next: record the key as root" \(dq;
.B \-\-yes
skips it and leaves the fingerprint confirmation, which runs as root,
untouched.
.TP
.B signer remove [\-\-repo \fIpath\fP] (\-\-file \fIpubkey\fP | \-\-key \fIline\fP) [\-\-yes]
(sudo) Remove that key's entries (key compromise must not be the
hand-edited path).
Its gate reads
.RB \(dq "next: remove the key as root" \(dq,
and
.B \-\-yes
skips that gate only.
.TP
.B signer list [\-\-repo \fIpath\fP]
Principals, key types, fingerprints.
.TP
.B signer path [\-\-repo \fIpath\fP]
The allowed\-signers file a signed tag of
.I path
verifies against, on stdout \(em the per\-repo override when one exists,
else the user tier's; without
.B \-\-repo
the user tier's alone.
Resolved the way the signature ceremony resolves it, so a consumer that
discovers signed releases (a deployer's signed-release offer) never has to
know where either file lives.
Prints nothing and exits 1 when no such file exists: no signers means
nothing verifies, which a consumer reads as no offer, never as an error.
Unprivileged, like
.BR "signer list" .
.SS Ignorable-key policy
.TP
.B ignorable add \fIkey\fP [\-\-under \fIdir\fP] [\-\-yes]
(sudo) Grant a dotted JSON key that
.B review \-\-file
may then declare ignored.
.B \-\-under
scopes the grant to that directory's subtree; omitted means
everywhere.
The pre-sudo gate reads
.RB \(dq "next: record the grant as root" \(dq;
.B \-\-yes
skips it and leaves the root-side confirmation untouched.
.TP
.B ignorable remove \fIkey\fP [\-\-under \fIdir\fP] [\-\-yes]
(sudo) Withdraw exactly that grant (same key, same scope).
Slots recording it fall back to byte-exact.
Its gate reads
.RB \(dq "next: withdraw the grant as root" \(dq,
and
.B \-\-yes
skips that gate only.
.TP
.B ignorable list
Machine tier, user tier and the effective intersection, each entry
with its scope.
.SS Inspection
.TP
.B status \fIrepo\fP|\fIfile\fP
Repo: pinned hash vs HEAD, the declared release name in its three
states (declared and live, rev-only, or stale \(em the live tag no
longer names the pin, so deploy will refuse), dirty-tree warning, and
an early warning when the checkout no longer holds the pinned rev
(rewritten history or gc \(em a deploy that needs to re-fetch it would
fail).
A record naming a former spelling of the path, or a slot directory
still wearing one, is reported as its own row rather than refused:
status is the repo's answer surface (verify covers files only), and
.B review
is the repair.
File: record vs live state.
.TP
.B show \fIfile\fP|\fIrepo\fP [\-\-algo \fIname\fP] [\-\-length \fIbits\fP]
Trusted re-display that records nothing \(em the rehearsal to
.BR review 's
ceremony, and never a substitute for it.
No sudo either way: it writes nothing, and root ownership of the
installed binary is what keeps the display path itself unswappable.
.RS
.PP
A
.I file
(anything that is not a directory) gets the non-repo ceremony: display
and hash one read, then print the hash plus a fail-closed wrapper.
Algorithm names: sha256, sha384, sha512, blake2b-256, blake2b-384,
blake2b-512, blake3
.RB ( \-\-length :
blake3 only, default 256).
blake2b and blake3 need b2sum / b3sum in the trusted PATH.
.PP
A
.I repo
(a directory) re-displays what its pin names: the pinned hash and
subject, the declared tag if the slot carries one, one line of
orientation on where live HEAD stands relative to the pin, and the
full tree at the pinned rev as a diff from the empty tree \(em read
from the object store, so a dirty work tree cannot alter a byte of it.
The display is of the pin, never of HEAD.
It goes through the same hardened git path the approval ceremony used,
which is the point: ambient
.BR git (1)
renders through repo-local diff drivers and textconv filters that any
writer of the checkout can install, so re-reading a pinned repo any
other way is not a re-read of what was approved.
.B \-\-algo
and
.B \-\-length
belong to the file form and are refused here.
.RE
.TP
.B list [\-\-under \fIdir\fP]
Live pins in your tier: decoded path, declared kind and full digest,
the declared release name if a repo slot carries one, the ignored\-key
declaration if a file slot has one, and custody state \(em a stored
approved copy, or the note that there is none.
.B \-\-under
restricts to paths below that directory.
.IP
Each pin is one block: the path alone on its first line, every fact
about it an indented \(dqlabel: value\(dq field under it, and a blank
line between entries.
That shape is also the grammar for anything parsing this listing: a
flush\-left line is a decoded absolute path and is the
.I whole
line, so a path holding spaces, tabs or glob metacharacters needs no
un\-quoting; every other non\-blank line begins with two spaces.
Digests are never abbreviated \(em the listing is meant to be read and
copied from, not re\-run.
.IP
A slot that violates the slot\-name invariant (see
.BR FILES )
carries a
.B note:
field naming the drift (a record naming another path than the
directory encodes) and the review that repairs it.
The note is one more indented field, so the grammar above holds.
A directory the volume aliases onto a former spelling of the live path
is not visible from a listing, which never asks about a live path;
.B status
reports that one.
.TP
.B slot \fIpath\fP
Print the slot directory for any path \(em repo or file, existing or
not; a name resolver for consumer symlinks and lane payloads, never an
existence answer.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the release version as
.RI \(dq pinned "\ " X.Y.Z \(dq
and exit 0.
The version is bumped in the release commit its tag points at, so a
checkout between releases reports the release it descends from.
.SS Installation
.TP
.B setup [\-\-yes]
(sudo) Provision the sudoers digest pin; self-install.
Shows the full plan and confirms before any authentication.
Refuses to write the sudoers entry unless the installed copy names an
absolute, root-owned interpreter on its first line: the digest pins the
script's bytes, not its interpreter, and
.BR sudo (8)
passes the caller's
.B PATH
through, so a
.I #!/usr/bin/env bash
shebang would let the invoker choose which shell runs as root.
The shipped script already names
.IR /bin/bash ,
so a verbatim install passes; the check is there for an install made
before the shebang was pinned, or one whose first line was hand-edited.
The nix module rewrites that line to the store bash at build time, which
satisfies the same rule.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Ceremonies exit 0 on success, 1 on usage or internal error, and 2 when
the human declines.
.PP
.B verify
answers:
.TS
l l.
0	ok
5	differs only in declared ignored keys
10	no slot
11	mismatch
13	tombstoned-but-present
17	record names another path (repair: review \-\-file)
20	pinned-but-missing
30	mode/owner violation
.TE
.PP
The owner half of 30 admits the tier user and root, plus any account the
build named in
.B security.pinned.rootOnlyOwners
\(em an account no user can act as, so its files are as unwritable to the
tier user as root's
.RB ( locked 's
module contributes its lock account
.IR _<user>\-lock ).
The list is baked in at build time; a manual install has none and refuses
such a file, unless the installer edits the
.B PINNED_ROOT_ONLY_OWNERS=
line in the copy before
.BR setup ,
which digest-pins the edited bytes.
.PP
.B cat
answers:
.TS
l l.
0	served
10	no record
13	tombstoned
16	no stored copy (review \-\-file \-\-store adds one)
20	the live file is missing
30	slot invariant violation
1	error
.TE
.PP
.B rev
answers:
.TS
l l.
0	printed
10	no slot
13	tombstoned
16	no declared release name (\-\-release only; rev\-only is a supported shape)
17	record names another path (repair: review \fIrepo\fP)
30	record ownership/mode violation
1	corrupt, malformed, or a file slot
.TE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/db/pinned
Root-owned record tree.
Each user's tier lives at
.IR /var/db/pinned/<user> ,
readable to that user through the
.I _<user>\-pinned
group.
.TP
.I /var/db/pinned/<user>/slots/<enc>/
One slot per approved path;
.I <enc>
is the path's percent\-encoding (print it with
.BR "pinned slot" ).
A slot holds exactly one state file:
.IR rev.git ,
.I pin.<algo>
or
.IR tombstone ;
more than one is a malformed slot that every verb refuses.
Both record kinds share one grammar, one line:
.IP
.EX
<hex>  <path>
.EE
.IP
the revision or digest, two spaces, the absolute path the record
covers \(em the line
.BR shasum (1)
.B \-c
reads, so a file record is also a check file.
The filename declares what the hex is, and a record whose hex is not
the declared length, or that names no path, is malformed.
.IP
The slot\-name invariant:
.I <enc>
is always the encoding of the path the record names.
The two are one fact spelled twice, and they drift only when a path is
respelled on a case\-insensitive volume (both spellings encode onto the
one directory).
.B review
maintains the invariant, repairing the record and the directory name at
entry (logged as
.BR respell );
.B status
and
.B list
report a violation wherever they find one.
.TP
.I /var/db/pinned/<user>/slots/<enc>/release
The slot's declared release name, one line.
Written by a review that carries one and by
.BR declare ,
cleared by a plain review and by
.BR "declare \-\-no\-release" ;
a deployer syncs the input's
.B ref=
to it after cross-checking that the live tag still names the pinned
rev.
.TP
.I /etc/pinned/ignorable.json
Machine tier of the ignorable-key policy.
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/pinned
Digest-pinned sudo grant, written by
.B setup
or by the nix module.
.TP
.I /usr/local/sbin/pinned
Default manual install target.
The nix module installs
.I /run/current-system/sw/bin/pinned
instead.
.SH APPROVAL HISTORY
Every ceremony logs through
.BR logger (1)
with tag
.BR pinned .
On macOS:
.PP
.nf
    log show \-\-predicate 'eventMessage CONTAINS "pinned:"'
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
Name the release an already-pinned rev is, so a deployer manages the
input's ref= from now on:
.PP
.nf
    pinned declare ~/Projects/some\-repo \-\-release v0.12.0
.fi
.PP
Pin a config file, keeping a served witness and tolerating one key:
.PP
.nf
    sudo pinned ignorable add statusLine.command
    pinned review \-\-file ~/.claude/settings.json \e
        \-\-ignore\-json\-key statusLine.command \-\-store
.fi
.PP
Gate a consumer on the approved bytes only:
.PP
.nf
    pinned verify \-\-emit /etc/thing.json | consumer \-\-stdin
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sudo (8),
.BR sudoers (5),
.BR git (1),
.BR ssh\-keygen (1),
